# SABOTAGE: T163 / ADR-051 R1 — the Meter stops upserting on the idempotency identity
# and does a plain insert. A replayed capture then hits the unique index: the caller
# gets an error instead of `{:ok, :duplicate}`, and — the sharper failure — a replay
# inside the caller's own transaction aborts it (Postgres 25P02 on every later
# statement), so metering poisons whatever request it rides in.
#
# Flips the replay test and the in-transaction test. The distinct-event POSITIVE
# CONTROL stays green (distinct keys never conflict), so the red is the dedup path,
# not the write path. The unique index itself is untouched: it still refuses a second
# row, which is why no double-count appears — only the broken replay contract.
# APP: demo
# TEST_FILES: test/usage_event_ledger_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: the same source_ref captured twice is recorded once, the replay answers :duplicate
# MUST_FAIL: a replay inside the caller's transaction does not abort it
diff --git a/samen_core/lib/samen/billing/meter.ex b/samen_core/lib/samen/billing/meter.ex
index 18e36a3..e4ddf11 100644
--- a/samen_core/lib/samen/billing/meter.ex
+++ b/samen_core/lib/samen/billing/meter.ex
@@ -83,12 +83,9 @@ defmodule Samen.Billing.Meter do
       }
 
       resource
-      |> Ash.Changeset.for_create(:record, attrs,
-        upsert?: true,
-        upsert_identity: :unique_idempotency_key,
-        upsert_fields: [],
-        authorize?: false
-      )
+      # SABOTAGE (ADR-051 R1): a plain insert — a replay now hits the unique index
+      # instead of upserting onto the existing row.
+      |> Ash.Changeset.for_create(:record, attrs, authorize?: false)
       |> Ash.Changeset.force_change_attribute(:id, id)
       |> Ash.Changeset.set_context(%{private: %{ChokepointGuard.marker_key() => true}})
       |> Ash.create(authorize?: false)
